Anterior Epistaxis
Common Infections and Afflictions
Unilateral nare bleeding, Visible anterior bleeding site, Easily controlled with pressure

VISIT CONTEXT
Case ID: CIA-009
Reason for Visit: Uncontrolled bleeding from the right nostril.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a patient with an active, continuous epistaxis that has persisted for 45 minutes despite the patient's attempts at home first aid. The prolonged nature of the bleeding and the patient's escalating anxiety regarding blood loss present an immediate risk of hemodynamic compromise or impending syncope. Urgent home assessment is clinically mandated to accurately assess cardiovascular stability, correctly apply targeted hemostatic measures (such as chemical cautery or localized packing), and definitively exclude a posterior bleed. Delaying this intervention until standard consulting hours is medically unsafe and poses a high risk of preventable deterioration, strictly satisfying the criteria for urgent after-hours MBS item numbers 585-600.

SUBJECTIVE
Chief Complaint: My nose just started gushing blood, and I've soaked through three towels trying to stop it.
History of Present Illness: Episode commenced approximately 45 minutes ago following an episode of vigorous nose blowing due to mild, pre-existing allergic rhinitis symptoms. The bleeding is strictly unilateral from the right nare. Patient reports exacerbation of bleeding due to dry indoor heating. Patient has attempted to control the bleeding by leaning backward and pinching the bridge of the nose, which has resulted in blood dripping down the back of the throat, causing nausea. Denies any history of easy bruising, prolonged bleeding from minor cuts, or recent trauma. No known history of hypertension or anticoagulant use.
Functional Status: Independent, but currently restricted by active bleeding and mild dizziness secondary to anxiety.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone. High situational distress.

OBJECTIVE
Vitals: BP: 145/90 mmHg | HR: 102 bpm | Temp: 36.6 C | RR: 18 bpm | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 80 Kg
General: Alert, visibly anxious, leaning backward while holding a blood-soaked towel to the face. Mild tachycardia consistent with acute distress.
Recent/Relevant Labs: None available. Normal complete blood count and coagulation profile would be expected in recurrent cases to assist in confirming the diagnosis, but are not acutely indicated for this primary presentation.
Physical Exam: Active, slow bleeding is visible from the right anterior nare. Upon clearing the nostril of partial clots, a prominent excoriated area with active oozing is visualized at Kiesselbach's plexus on the anterior nasal septum. Application of firm, direct pressure to the cartilaginous portion of the nose immediately arrests the bleeding. Examination of the posterior oropharynx reveals no active blood flow down the posterior pharyngeal wall while pressure is applied. Skin examination shows an absolute absence of bruising or petechiae elsewhere.
Environmental Observations: Central heating is running on high, resulting in distinctly dry ambient air.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular medications. No recent NSAID or aspirin use.

ASSESSMENT
Primary Diagnosis: Anterior Epistaxis - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:
Posterior Epistaxis - Ruled out as bleeding is immediately and completely controlled with isolated anterior pressure, and no active bleeding is visualized in the posterior pharynx during anterior compression.
Systemic Coagulopathy - Unlikely due to the lack of concurrent physical signs (petechiae, purpura) and a negative personal/family history of bleeding disorders.
Nasal Foreign Body - Definitively ruled out by direct visual inspection of the anterior nasal cavity.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY
The patient presented with a prolonged, persistent epistaxis driven by incorrect first aid technique (pinching the bony bridge and tilting the head back), which caused significant distress and nausea. Urgent on-site assessment was essential to correctly evaluate airway protection, rule out a life-threatening posterior arterial bleed, and rapidly achieve hemostasis. Direct physical examination confirmed a benign anterior bleed from Kiesselbach's plexus. By demonstrating and enforcing the correct physical compression technique, clinical hemostasis was quickly achieved, successfully averting an unnecessary emergency department transfer. This definitive intervention could not be safely delayed until the following day, fulfilling the criteria for MBS 585-600.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN
Clinical Interventions: Bleeding arrested via 15 minutes of uninterrupted, firm compression of the lower cartilaginous nose combined with topical application of a vasoconstrictor (oxymetazoline spray) on a cotton pledget. Chemical cautery with silver nitrate was not required but remains an option for immediate recurrence.
Positive Behavioral Change: To prevent the newly formed clot from dislodging, open your mouth wide whenever you need to sneeze. Sneezing with a closed mouth directs high-pressure air through the nasal cavity, which will violently blow out the clot and immediately restart the bleeding.
Home-Based Services: None required.
DME/Safety: Consider acquiring a cool-mist humidifier for the bedroom.
Follow-up: Review with regular General Practitioner in 7 days if bleeding recurs.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN
Present immediately to the Emergency Department or call 000 if bleeding restarts and cannot be stopped after 20 minutes of firm, correct pressure, or if you feel faint, dizzy, or begin vomiting large amounts of blood.

PATIENT INFORMATION
Disclaimer: Diagnosis is based on direct visualization; recurrent or atypical bleeds may require formal ENT evaluation.
Clinical Course: The site will remain fragile for 7-10 days. The risk of re-bleeding is highest in the first 48 hours while the mucosal lining heals over the clot.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE
Seek immediate review if bleeding restarts heavily, or if you notice unexplained bruising developing on your arms or legs over the next week.

PATIENT EDUCATION
Treatment Explanation: The bleeding came from a fragile web of blood vessels near the front of the nose, triggered by dry air and vigorous blowing. Pinching the soft part of the nose acts as a physical tourniquet to stop the flow, allowing a permanent scab to form.
Actionable Steps: Keep your head elevated above your heart for the rest of the day. Avoid hot showers, hot beverages, and strenuous exercise for 24 hours, as heat and increased blood pressure dilate the blood vessels and invite re-bleeding. Apply a thin layer of petroleum jelly to the inside of the nostril twice daily using a cotton swab to keep the healing tissue moist.

